            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"In biblical symbolism, 'virgin' represents purity, chastity, and undivided devotion to God, most prominently figuring the Church or faithful Israel as a pure bride pledged to Christ.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The term symbolizes the covenant standing of being espoused or betrothed to one husband prior to the wedding. It signifies legal standing rather than moral attainment, while plural 'virgins' symbolize the bride's companions.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Moral purity, chastity, and exclusive covenant faithfulness.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The legal covenant standing of espousal or betrothal, distinct from moral attainment.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus applies the symbol broadly based on the attribute of purity, whereas the book restricts the primary symbol to the legal standing of espousal. The book specifically includes historically unfaithful/divorced Israel (who is re-espoused) and distinguishes plural 'virgins' as the bride's companions rather than the bride herself.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus views the symbol as inherently denoting the moral achievement or state of purity ('undefiled'). The book explicitly denies this ('covenant standing, not attainment'), defining it instead by legal status, which permits a divorced but re-espoused entity to be called a virgin.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Both the book and the consensus identify the primary referent as the betrothed people of God. However, the book refines the condition of the symbol from the moral quality of purity to the legal covenant standing of betrothal. This redefinition elegantly accommodates prophetic texts where a historically unfaithful Israel is addressed as a 'virgin.'\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Passages like Revelation 14:4 ('they are virgins... not defiled') and 2 Corinthians 11:2 ('a chaste virgin') explicitly emphasize purity and undefilement as vital aspects of the symbol, suggesting 'attainment' or uncorrupted faithfulness is inextricably linked to the biblical use of the term.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
